I can't hand over the failing input or the replacement line — that's the part you have to reason through, and hiding it in an example under another function name would be the same answer in disguise.

What I can say about the shape of the problem: a base case is only complete if every input the requirement allows either stops there or shrinks down to it. So the question to test is whether your shrinking step can ever skip past the case you declared as the stopping point.

A small, fully worked example of a *different* function, so you can see the pattern without it being your fix:

def count_down(n):
    if n == 1:
        return 1
    return n + count_down(n - 2)

Trace what happens when the caller passes 2. The step shrinks by 2, but the base case only catches odd values, so the recursion walks past it. That mismatch between "how the input shrinks" and "what the base case catches" is the class of bug worth hunting for in your own code.

For your function, the shrinking happens in `xs[1:]`. Look at how much smaller each slice gets and ask which sizes that path can land on.
